    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d260">

                <pb facs="0508" n="490" xml:id="pg490"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.51G/9–1151</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Joint Chiefs of Staff to the Secretary
                        of Defense</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Marshall</hi>)<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d260-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-08-31">31 August 1951</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#flush">Subject: Washington Foreign Ministers Meetings (Tripartite
                    Talks) Draft Position Paper (WFMT–13<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>, Dated 25
                    August 1951) Entitled “Additional Aid to Indochina.”<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d260-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>The Joint Chiefs of Staff have reviewed the Department of State draft position
                    paper, subject as above, and concur in those parts of the paper having military
                    implications, subject to the following:</p>
                <p>Change subparagraph C under paragraph II, <hi rendition="#italic">U.S.
                        Objective</hi>, to read (changes indicated in the usual manner):</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“C. To make clear that U.S. aid to Indochina is <del
                            rendition="#strikethrough">necessarily</del>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">among other things</hi> conditioned by U.S. global
                        commitments and available resources <del rendition="#strikethrough"
                            >and</del>
                        <del rendition="#strikethrough">manpower</del>.”</p>
                </quote>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Reason:</hi> For clarity and to avoid any possible
                    implication that United States armed forces might be committed to Indochina.</p>
                <closer>For the Joint Chiefs of Staff: <lb/>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">General Omar</hi> N. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Bradley</hi>
                    </signed>
                    <lb/>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Chairman, Joint Chiefs of Staff</hi>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d260-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d260-f1-content">
                <p>In a letter of September 11 transmitting this memorandum to Secretary of State
                    Acheson, Secretary Marshall indicated that it represented the position of the
                    Department of Defense. (611.51G/9–1151)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d260-f2" n="2" xml:id="d260-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed. Regarding the Washington Foreign Ministers meetings, see footnote 2,
                        <ref target="#pg491">p. 491</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
